<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Welser family</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as House of Welser</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Welser were a family of merchants and bankers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augsburg">Augsburg</a>, in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, among the richest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> in the 16th century alongside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fugger-family">Fuggers</a>. They traded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spice-trade">spices</a>, textiles and metals from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antwerp">Antwerp</a> and lent large sums to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a>. In return, in 1528 he granted them the right to colonise and govern the province of Venezuela, the colony known as Klein-Venedig, which they held until the 1540s.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/welser-family.jpg" alt="Welser family" width="320" height="511" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Samhanin · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coat_of_arms_of_Welser_family.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The family, which claimed descent from the Byzantine general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belisarius">Belisarius</a>, can be traced to the 13th century, when its members held official posts in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augsburg">Augsburg</a>. In the 15th century the brothers Bartholomew and Lucas Welser traded widely with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a> and elsewhere, with branches in the main trading centres of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antwerp">Antwerp</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a>, and branches of the family settled at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg">Nuremberg</a> and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>. Lucas's son Antony, who died in 1518, was one of the first Germans to use the sea route to the East opened by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasco-da-gama">Vasco da Gama</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>By the Contract of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a> of 1528, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> rewarded the Welsers for financing his election in 1519 with privileges in the African slave trade and the conquest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>, and Bartholomeus Welser V received Venezuela as security for a large loan. From 1528 to 1556 seven expeditions plundered and exploited local societies, and the first governors, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ambrosius-ehinger">Ambrosius Alfinger</a>, Nicolas Federmann and Georg von Speyer, captured and enslaved indigenous people after failing to find gold. Bartholomeus's son Bartholomeus VI and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philipp-von-hutten">Philipp von Hutten</a> were executed at El Tocuyo by the Spanish governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-de-carvajal">Juan de Carvajal</a> in 1546, the year the family lost its rule.</p><p>The Welsers had begun <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> production in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santo-domingo">Santo Domingo</a> by 1523, and in the following years thousands of enslaved Africans were transported to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>. They also found <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a>. Bartholomeus Welser V was ennobled in 1532.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippine-welser">Philippine Welser</a>, daughter of Franz Welser, secretly married Archduke Ferdinand, second son of Emperor Ferdinand I, and their children were barred from the rank of archduke; Markus Welser, a humanist and historian, became mayor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augsburg">Augsburg</a> in 1611. Like the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fugger-family">Fuggers</a>, the family stayed Catholic after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a>. The Augsburg main line died out in 1797 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg">Nuremberg</a> branch in 1878, while the Ulm branch, imperial barons from 1713, survives and administers the Welser Family Foundation, founded on 1 April 1539.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Welser family” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1528 Charles V granted the <b>Welser family</b> of Augsburg the right to colonise and govern the province of Venezuela in return for loans.</blockquote><a href="/state/klein-venedig">Klein-Venedig</a></li><li><blockquote>Spain founded Coro in 1527, and from 1528 the province was leased to the <b>Welser</b> bankers as Klein-Venedig.</blockquote><a href="/state/province-of-venezuela">Province of Venezuela</a></li></ul></section>
